  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed (i.e., has a higher probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). To assess skewness: Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 100% probability, so it has no variance and thus no skewness—it is a degenerate distribution. Lottery B offers 4000 Euros with 80% probability and 0 Euros with 20% probability. Since the higher outcome (4000) has a higher probability (80%), it is left-skewed (favourable outcome is more likely), not right-skewed. A right-skewed lottery would have a small probability of a high outcome and a high probability of a low outcome, which is not the case here. Therefore, Lottery B is actually left-skewed, and Lottery A has zero skewness. According to the decision reason, which prefers more right-skewed lotteries, neither lottery fits that preference, but A would be less right-skewed (or more left-skewed or neutral) than B. However, since B is not right-skewed either, the reason would not clearly prefer either, but technically would not prefer B. The information needed (outcomes and probabilities) is available, so the reason can be applied to the problem.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'Better to take the safe win.' This indicates that the individual values certainty and prefers a guaranteed outcome over a risky one. The individual considers the sureness of winning in Lottery A (100% chance of 3000 Euros) as the main factor in their decision. There is no mention of probabilities being assigned to good or bad outcomes in terms of distribution shape, nor any discussion of skewness, asymmetry, or comparative likelihood of high vs. low outcomes beyond the basic certainty of A. The justification is based purely on risk aversion and the appeal of a guaranteed payoff.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, particularly favoring lotteries that are more right-skewed (high chance of bad outcomes, low chance of good ones), which is not the case for either lottery here. The individual, however, does not discuss or reference any concept related to the shape of the outcome distribution, probability weighting, or skewness. Instead, the justification is based on preferring certainty (a safe win), which aligns more with risk aversion or a certainty effect, not skewness preference. The information considered by the individual (certainty vs. risk) does not overlap with the information required for the skewness reason (distribution asymmetry). The justification (safe win) also does not align with the rule of the decision reason (prefer more right-skewed). In fact, if anything, the individual prefers the less risky, certain option, while the skewness reason would favor more right-skewed (riskier in the bad direction) options, which is contrary. Therefore, there is no evidence the individual used the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

